 ],
 "text": "Tonight, every mosquito on Earth drops dead. Most of nature barely notices, but one flower will. Night one, no wine at your ear. Only females bite. They need your blood for their eggs. Tonight, you sleep without a single bite. Week one, the Arctic summer swarms are gone. Up to 10,000, once fed on one caribou at a time. Now the herds graze, instead of running. Month one, pond fish and dragonflies just eat something else. But in northern bugs, one orchid waits. Its main pollinator was the mosquito. Without them, it may stop making seeds. Wait, doesn't chocolate need mosquitoes? No, cacao is pollinated by tiny midges. Your chocolate is safe. Year one, malaria killed 610,000 people in 2024. Most were children under 5. Dengue and Zika lose their main carrier too. Over half a million people a year get to live. Year 10, empty niches don't stay empty. One expert predicted a hiccup, then life moves on. Something else moves in, better or worse. Some say they killed half of all humans who ever lived. Critics call that far too high. So would you press the button. Tonight every mosquito on Earth drops dead."
}